; common-sense is what anyone knows before studying anything.
; not physics, not mathematics — the ground of everyday experience.
; a ball falls. water is wet. a heavy thing is hard to lift.
; fire is hot. ice is cold. things wear out over time.
; 
; this domain is the bridge between ordinary language and formal knowing.
; when someone says "the ball moves fast" they are not citing Newton.
; they are pointing at something they have seen a thousand times.
; the pipeline must meet them there — recognise the pointing — before
; it can ask whether physics is relevant.
; 
; common-sense nodes do not have mantras. they have swarupa (nature)
; and sambandha (connections to formal domains). they are the
; entry points through which everyday language finds its way into
; the graph. once recognised, physics or chemistry or biology
; can take over and give the formal structure.
; 
; the three pillars of common-sense:
; objects  — things that exist, can be pointed at, can be named
; qualities — properties that are directly perceived (fast, heavy, hot)
; events   — things that happen (falls, collides, melts)

(kosha domain-common-sense
  (sthita domain-kosha)
  (yukta physics biology language meta))
